{
  "abstract": "Let an ordinary no-three-in-line set in a translated integer square of side $n$ be supported on a union of parabolas, each reduced at its own prime. For two distinct primes at least $n$ we prove an upper bound of $3n/2+o(n)$. For three such primes the bound is $7n/4+o(n)$, uniformly in the equations, orientations, translations and prime sizes. On a suitable cardinal axis at most one parabola is a graph. Each remaining projection has a linear discriminant. The Chinese remainder theorem and Burgess's bound count lines missed simultaneously at distinct primes. For two nonsingular conics at comparable distinct primes, a board of side at least twice the smaller prime has the same $7n/4+o(n)$ ceiling whenever both equations have degree two along a common axis. In an injective window a second-moment estimate bounds the number of exceptional relative translations. At consecutive primes $p<q$ congruent to three modulo four, a crossed pair of parabolas supplies $(2-o(1))n$ sites satisfying both axial capacities on side $n=2p$. For every fixed $\\eta>0$, either unit diagonal direction alone bounds the selectable set by $(7/4+\\eta)n$ outside $O_\\eta(p)$ of the $(p-1)(q-1)$ coefficient pairs. This follows by counting the sites on physical diagonals that meet both field parabolas. The unrestricted asymptotic construction remains open.",
